# SYNOPSIS

```
src
dst
wdir
type
name=value name='a value'
ndata
data
```

```
# a rules file
addr=':(#?[0-9]+):?'

type is text
data matches '([.a-zA-Z0-9_/\-]*[a-zA-Z0-9_/\-])('$addr')?'
arg isfile $1
data set $file
attr add addr=$3
plumb to edit
plumb client window $editor
```

# DESCRIPTION

The plumber routes data between programs by rules the user writes (07 §7).
Its messages and its rule language are 9front's, kept as 9front has them; `lib/vx-plumb` packs, parses and matches them, and the plumber and its clients use it.

## The message

A plumb message is seven fields, each ended by a newline but the last: the program that sent it (`src`), the port it is for (`dst`), the directory a file name in it is relative to (`wdir`), the kind of data (`type`, almost always `text`), its attributes (`attr`), the number of bytes of data in decimal (`ndata`), and the data itself, which alone may hold newlines.
A field left out is an empty line, and `ndata` must count the data exactly, 0 for none.

Attributes are `name=value` pairs separated by blanks.
A value that holds a blank, a quote or an `=` is written in quotes, `'`, with a quote inside written twice, as in rc(1); one cannot hold a newline.
A malformed attribute ends the list.
`click=N` says the data is text around a click, N runes into it; the rules choose what the user meant from it (below).

## Rules

A rules file is a sequence of rulesets, each ended by a blank line; a line starting with `#` is a comment and counts as blank.
Each ruleset is patterns, then actions, each a line of an object, a verb and an argument separated by blanks.
A ruleset needs at least one of each, but one made only of `plumb to` lines declares those ports and does nothing else.

The plumber tries the rulesets in order on each message written to it.
The first whose patterns all match is applied and the rest are not tried.
If none matches, the message is refused unless its `dst` names a port, from which it is sent unchanged.

The objects of patterns are the message's fields, `src`, `dst`, `wdir`, `type`, `data` and `attr`, and `arg`, the rule's own argument.
Their verbs:

: `is`
  The object's text is the argument, exactly.
: `matches`
  The object's whole text matches the argument, a regular expression.
  The expression is sam's (leftmost-longest, as 9front's libregexp is): literal runes, `.` (not a newline), `[]` and `[^]` classes with ranges, `*`, `+`, `?`, `|`, parentheses, `^`, `$` and `\` escapes.
  `$0` is the match, `$1` to `$9` the text of its parenthesised parts.
  `data matches` on a message with a `click` attribute takes the leftmost-longest match that holds the click instead of the whole data; each `data matches` of the ruleset must then select the same text, and when the ruleset applies, the data becomes that text and the `click` attribute goes.
: `isfile`, `isdir`
  The object, `data`, `wdir` or `arg`, made absolute by `wdir` and cleaned, names a file that is not a directory, or a directory: `$file` or `$dir` is that name.
: `set`
  The object becomes the argument. It always matches.
: `add`
  For `attr`: the argument's attributes are added to the message's. It always matches.
: `delete`
  For `attr`: the attribute the argument names is deleted; the pattern fails if the message has none. (9front's page says it matches anyway; its code, kept here, does not.)

Patterns run in order, and what one rewrites stays rewritten, so rewriting comes after matching.
The object of actions is `plumb`, and their verbs:

: `to`
  The port to send the message from: its `dst`. A message with a `dst` already skips a ruleset for another port.
: `start`
  When no program has the port open, the argument is a command to run; the message is thrown away.
: `client`
  As `start`, but the message is held until the command opens the port.

A ruleset has at most one `start` or `client`.

An argument is read as rc reads a word: `'...'` quotes, with `''` a quote inside, and `$name` substitutes a variable.
A line `name=value` between rulesets sets a variable, whose value's quotes and variables are taken in at once.
Besides those, `$0` to `$9` (above), `$src`, `$dst`, `$wdir`, `$type`, `$data`, `$attr` (the attributes as one line), `$file` and `$dir` (an `isfile`'s and `isdir`'s, or the data made absolute by `wdir`) are the message's, while a message is matched.
A `$` naming nothing stays as it is.
A `start` or `client` argument is split into the command's words at its unquoted blanks before substitution, so `$1` is one word, whatever it holds.

`include FILE` reads FILE in its place, from `/lib/plumb` when FILE is not there and is neither rooted nor `./` or `../`; includes nest 10 deep.

The ports are every `plumb to` the rules have named; once made, a port stays though the rules change.
The ports `.`, `..`, `send` and `rules` cannot be named.

# EXAMPLES

9front's rule for a file name and an address, which `/lib/plumb/fileaddr` defines as `$addr`: a click on `src/main.c:12` in text from `/usr/glenda` sends the message on as

```
plumb
edit
/usr/glenda
text
addr=12
22
/usr/glenda/src/main.c
```

A manual page named in text, `vxui(2)`, as 9front's `basic` names it:

```
type is text
data matches '([a-zA-Z0-9_\-./]+)\(([1-9])\)'
plumb start rc -c 'man -b '$2' '$1
```

